// Review questions for the side quests' concepts (zone 98): the North Road, the town side quests, Explodia's SRE
// quests and the Isles. Each concept gets three here on top of the questions its quest asks. They stay out of dungeon
// pools, and town reviews ask them once you've met the concept (S.reviewable and S.questionsFor in js/state.js).
SD.questions.push(
  // chunksync: Chunked File Sync
  { id: 'rv-chunksync1', c: 'chunksync', type: 'mc', q: 'Mobile users upload 4 GB videos over flaky networks. About a third of uploads drop partway, and every retry starts again from zero. What fixes this?', opts: ['Send the file in chunks and, after a drop, upload only the ones still missing', 'Raise the server\'s request timeout so slow uploads are not cut off midway', 'Gzip each video on the phone first so every attempt has fewer bytes to send', 'Retry the whole upload with exponential backoff and jitter between attempts'], a: 0, no: [null, 'These uploads die because the connection drops, not because the server gives up. With a longer timeout, a drop still restarts from zero.', 'Video is already compressed, so gzip barely shrinks it, and a drop still restarts the whole upload. Gzip pays off on text like JSON and logs.', 'Backoff with jitter spaces retries out so they don\'t stampede the server, but each retry still resends all 4 GB. It changes when you retry, not what.'], why: 'With chunked uploads the client asks which chunks the server already has and sends only the rest, so a drop at 80% costs only the chunk in flight, not the 3.2 GB already sent. S3 multipart uploads and the tus resumable upload protocol work this way.' },
  { id: 'rv-chunksync2', c: 'chunksync', type: 'mc', q: 'A sync client splits files into fixed 4 MB chunks. When a user inserts a line near the start of a 500 MB file, almost every chunk re-uploads. Why, and what fixes it?', opts: ['The chunk hash is too weak; moving from MD5 to SHA-256 stops the false changes', 'The chunks are too large; cutting them to 64 KB means each edit touches less data', 'Every later boundary shifts; pick boundaries by content with a rolling hash', 'Uploads go through app servers; presigned URLs would send chunks straight to storage'], a: 2, no: ['A weak hash would make different chunks look the same and skip uploads, not add them. These chunks really did change: each now holds shifted bytes.', 'Smaller fixed chunks still all shift after an insert, so they all change too. Small chunks help in-place edits, at the cost of far more metadata.', null, 'Presigned URLs take app servers off the upload path, but the same chunks still travel. They change where the bytes go, not how many.'], why: 'With fixed offsets, inserting bytes pushes every later chunk boundary along, so every later chunk hash changes. Content-defined chunking uses a rolling hash (such as a Rabin fingerprint) to cut wherever the bytes match a pattern, so boundaries move with the content and only the chunks around the edit change.' },
  { id: 'rv-chunksync3', c: 'chunksync', type: 'mc', q: 'A file sync service uses 4 MB content-hashed chunks. To shrink uploads after small edits, a team proposes 4 KB chunks instead. What is the main cost?', opts: ['Less dedupe, since small chunks are less likely to match across files', 'Hash collisions become likely once there are trillions of tiny chunks', 'Each small edit re-uploads more data, since a change now spans more chunks', 'About 1,000 times more chunk hashes per file to store, look up and send'], a: 3, no: ['Smaller chunks find more matches, not fewer, since shared runs of bytes line up more often. Dedupe is one of the things that improves.', 'With SHA-256, a collision even among trillions of chunks is so unlikely that it is ignored in practice. Chunk size does not change that.', 'A small edit now touches a few 4 KB chunks instead of one 4 MB chunk, so fewer bytes travel. Upload size is the part that gets better.', null], why: 'A 1 GB file is 256 chunks at 4 MB but about 262,000 at 4 KB. Each one needs a hash in the file\'s metadata, a check against the store and an entry in the list other devices fetch, so metadata and request overhead balloon. Sync services settle on a few MB as the middle ground.' },
  // blobmeta: Metadata DB + Blob Store
  { id: 'rv-blobmeta1', c: 'blobmeta', type: 'mc', q: 'A file app\'s API servers stream every upload through to S3. Their CPUs are mostly idle, but their network links are saturated and uploads queue. Best fix?', opts: ['Switch the upload endpoint from REST to gRPC streaming to cut overhead', 'Return a short-lived presigned URL so the client uploads straight to S3', 'Put a CDN in front of the API so uploads are cached at nearby edge locations', 'Store uploads as BLOBs in the database so the API skips the S3 hop'], a: 1, no: ['gRPC trims per-message overhead, but every byte still crosses the API servers. It suits typed service-to-service calls.', null, 'CDNs cache downloads near users. Uploads are writes, so they still have to reach your API servers.', 'The bytes would still cross the API servers, then weigh down the database too. The database holds metadata; big bytes belong in object storage.'], why: 'Your server signs a URL for one object, one action and a few minutes, and the client sends the bytes straight to the blob store. The API servers only hand out URLs and record metadata, so the heavy traffic never touches them.' },
  { id: 'rv-blobmeta2', c: 'blobmeta', type: 'mc', q: 'Clients upload chunks to S3 with presigned URLs, then commit the file\'s chunk list to the metadata DB. Some clients crash between the two steps. What is the result?', opts: ['Other devices see the new file version but fail when fetching its chunks', 'S3 rolls back the chunks, since their upload was never committed to the DB', 'The two stores now disagree, so they need a two-phase commit to stay in step', 'Chunks no file points to pile up, so a sweeper must clear them out later'], a: 3, no: ['The new version appears only once its chunk list is committed, which never happened. Committing metadata before the chunks exist is what causes that.', 'S3 knows nothing of your database: an uploaded object stays until something deletes it. No transaction spans S3 and your DB.', 'S3 can\'t take part in a two-phase commit. Writing bytes first and committing last already keeps files correct; what remains is cleanup.', null], why: 'Committing last means no file ever points at missing bytes. The price is orphaned chunks, which cost storage but break nothing. A background job deletes chunks no file references once they pass a grace period, so it leaves alone chunks a recent upload may still commit.' },
  { id: 'rv-blobmeta3', c: 'blobmeta', type: 'mc', q: 'A file-sharing app gives out presigned download URLs. To cut API calls, a PM wants each URL to last a week instead of 15 minutes. What is the main cost?', opts: ['Anyone the link reaches can download until it expires, even after unsharing', 'Every URL must be stored server-side so the blob store can verify it later', 'The signature weakens over time, so a week-long URL can be brute forced', 'Each download makes the blob store check sharing rules with your API first'], a: 0, no: [null, 'A presigned URL carries its own expiry and signature, which the blob store checks with no lookup. Nothing is stored per URL.', 'A signature is as hard to forge on day seven as in minute one. The risk is the link being forwarded, not cracked.', 'The blob store never calls your API; it checks only the signature and expiry. That is exactly why it can\'t know you revoked access.'], why: 'A presigned URL is a bearer token: whoever holds it gets the file, and the blob store never sees your sharing rules. Keep URLs short-lived and have share links go through your API, which checks access and signs a fresh URL each time.' },
  // chatdelivery: Chat Delivery and Receipts
  { id: 'rv-chatdelivery1', c: 'chatdelivery', type: 'mc', q: 'A user taps send in a tunnel. The message reaches the server, but the ack back is lost, so the app retries and the friend sees it twice. What is the standard fix?', opts: ['Rely on TCP, which already delivers every byte exactly once and in order', 'Have the friend\'s app hide identical texts that arrive within a few seconds', 'Give each message an ID made on the phone; the server drops repeats', 'Stop auto-retrying, and let the user tap resend if the tick never appears'], a: 2, no: ['TCP dedupes only within one connection. The retry goes out on a new connection after the drop, so to TCP it is brand new data.', 'Real repeats like "ok", "ok" would vanish. Matching on content guesses at identity; an ID states it.', null, 'A manual resend is still a retry, so the duplicate still happens, just later. Retries are fine once the server can recognise them.'], why: 'The phone makes a unique ID per message and reuses it on every retry; the server remembers recent IDs per sender and ignores repeats. It is the same idea as an idempotency key on a POST request, which is not idempotent by itself.' },
  { id: 'rv-chatdelivery2', c: 'chatdelivery', type: 'mc', q: 'A chat server holding 500,000 WebSocket connections crashes. Messages are stored before they are pushed. What happens to messages sent to those users in the next 30 seconds?', opts: ['They are lost, since the registry still routes those users to the dead server', 'They wait in storage with one tick, and arrive when the users reconnect', 'Senders see two ticks, since the registry still lists those users as online', 'Other chat servers take over the open sockets and deliver them right away'], a: 1, no: ['Storing first means a failed push loses nothing. The message stays undelivered until the user reconnects and pulls it.', null, 'Two ticks need an ack from the recipient\'s device, not a registry entry. With the device unreachable, no ack comes back.', 'A WebSocket is a TCP connection to one machine and dies with it. Clients must reconnect, usually through the load balancer to a healthy server.'], why: 'The messages are already saved (one tick), so the failed push costs only time. The phones detect the lost connection, reconnect to another server, which updates the registry, and fetch everything after their last acked sequence number. Reconnects need jittered backoff so 500,000 phones don\'t all arrive at once.' },
  { id: 'rv-chatdelivery3', c: 'chatdelivery', type: 'mc', q: 'A chat service orders messages with one global sequence number for all chats, handed out by a single counter service. At a million messages a second, what is the main problem?', opts: ['A 64-bit counter would run out within a few years at that message rate', 'Messages would show out of order whenever the network delivers them late', 'It gives no total order, so group chats would still need vector clocks', 'Every message waits on one counter, though order only matters within a chat'], a: 3, no: ['2^63 at a million a second lasts about 290,000 years. Counter size is fine; the single point every message waits on is not.', 'Clients sort by the number, so arrival order doesn\'t matter; that is what the numbers are for.', 'One counter does give a total order: every message gets one place in line. Vector clocks are for spotting concurrent writes when there is no single counter.', null], why: 'One counter for the whole service is a throughput cap and a single point of failure, all to order messages nobody compares across chats. Give each chat its own sequence, assigned by the server that owns that chat, so chats scale out independently.' },
  // netfaults: Unreliable Networks and Timeouts
  { id: 'rv-netfaults1', c: 'netfaults', type: 'mc', q: 'Heartbeats between two regions usually take 80 ms but spike to 2 s when links congest. A fixed 500 ms timeout keeps declaring healthy nodes dead. What fits best?', opts: ['Send heartbeats over TCP so a lost beat is retransmitted, not missed', 'Set the timeout from measured heartbeat delays and how much they vary', 'Move membership into ZooKeeper, which detects failure without timeouts', 'Drop heartbeats; have each node announce when it is about to go down'], a: 1, no: ['The problem is late beats, not lost ones, and a retransmitted beat arrives later still. TCP promises reliable delivery, not timely delivery.', null, 'ZooKeeper sessions expire on a timeout too. Every failure detector on an unreliable network comes down to a timeout; the question is how long.', 'A planned shutdown can announce itself, but a crash, a hang or a cut link sends nothing. Silence is exactly the case you must detect.'], why: 'Delay varies, so any fixed timeout is too short on bad days or too slow on good ones. Detectors like the phi accrual detector in Cassandra and Akka track the spread of recent heartbeat delays and suspect a node only when its silence is unlikely given that history.' },
  { id: 'rv-netfaults2', c: 'netfaults', type: 'mc', q: 'Two database replicas accept writes and settle conflicts by keeping the write with the later local timestamp. Replica B\'s clock runs 300 ms behind. What can go wrong?', opts: ['A write on B just after one on A can lose to it and silently vanish', 'Nothing serious, as long as both replicas sync their clocks with NTP', 'Both writes are kept as siblings, and the app merges them on next read', 'Reads on B are 300 ms stale for a while, then the replicas converge'], a: 0, no: [null, 'NTP keeps skew small but never zero, and a bad sync can jump a clock. Writes closer together than the remaining skew still misorder.', 'Keeping both as siblings needs version vectors, as Dynamo-style stores like Riak offer. Last write wins keeps one value and throws the other away.', 'They converge, but on the wrong value: the dropped write never comes back. The damage is permanent, not a short lag.'], why: 'If B writes 100 ms after A, B\'s timestamp still reads 200 ms earlier, so last write wins keeps A\'s older value and drops B\'s with no error. Local clocks can\'t order events across machines; order writes with a logical clock or a single leader instead.' },
  { id: 'rv-netfaults3', c: 'netfaults', type: 'mc', q: 'A call has a 40 ms median and a 120 ms p99. To fail fast, a team sets a 50 ms timeout with 3 immediate retries. What happens when that service slows down?', opts: ['Users see more errors, but the load on the slow service stays flat', 'Little: a timed-out call is abandoned, so the server stops work on it', 'Slow but working calls get retried, so load on the service can quadruple', 'Retries land on other replicas, which spreads the load so it recovers'], a: 2, no: ['Every timeout fires a new request while the old one still runs, so load climbs as the service slows. That feedback loop is a retry storm.', 'The server usually keeps working after the caller gives up, so retries pile on top. Some RPC stacks, like gRPC, pass deadlines along so servers can stop early.', null, 'The extra requests still land on the same slow fleet. Backoff with jitter, a retry budget or a circuit breaker gives it room to recover.'], why: 'A 50 ms timeout already cuts off normal calls between the median and the p99. When the service slows, most calls time out and each becomes up to four requests that all keep running. Set timeouts above normal worst-case latency, retry with backoff, and cap retries.' },
  // datamodels: Document vs Relational
  { id: 'rv-datamodels1', c: 'datamodels', type: 'mc', q: 'A marketplace has listings in 300 categories: a guitar has a string count, a sofa has fabric and dimensions, and new attributes appear weekly. Each listing page is read whole. Which storage fits best?', opts: ['One relational table with a nullable column for every attribute', 'A columnar warehouse, since listings have so many different columns', 'A document per listing, each with its own set of fields', 'A graph database, with each attribute an edge to a value vertex'], a: 2, no: ['Hundreds of mostly empty columns, and a migration every week as attributes appear. A fixed relational schema suits rows that share one shape.', 'Columnar warehouses (OLAP) scan a few columns across millions of rows, for analytics. Loading one listing whole is the opposite access pattern.', null, 'Graphs suit many-to-many links followed over several hops. Reading one listing would become a traversal with nothing to traverse.'], why: 'Each listing is a self-contained tree read in one go, and the shape differs by category, so a document store\'s flexible schema (schema on read) and single read fit. Many relational databases offer the same through a JSON column, like Postgres JSONB.' },
  { id: 'rv-datamodels2', c: 'datamodels', type: 'mc', q: 'A blog stores each post as one document with its comments embedded in an array. One post goes viral and collects 60,000 comments, and its page crawls. What change fixes it?', opts: ['Shard the posts collection by post ID across more servers', 'Add read replicas so the big post is served from more servers', 'Move posts into a graph database, with comments as edges', 'Store each comment as its own record keyed by post ID, read in pages'], a: 3, no: ['A document lives whole on one shard, so the giant post stays giant. Sharding spreads many documents, not one big one.', 'Replicas share out the number of reads, but each read still loads every comment. They help with read volume, not read size.', 'Comments are a one-to-many list read newest first, not a network to traverse. Graphs suit many-to-many links followed over several hops.', null], why: 'Embed children only when they are few and read together. An unbounded list makes every page view load every comment, and some stores cap a document\'s size (MongoDB at 16 MB). Keep comments as separate records indexed by post and time, and page through them with a cursor.' },
  { id: 'rv-datamodels3', c: 'datamodels', type: 'mc', q: 'A team chose a document store so they would never need schema migrations. Three years and many app versions later, what cost of schema on read are they paying?', opts: ['Every read joins several collections to rebuild one record', 'Code that reads must handle every shape ever written', 'Each new field locks the collection while old documents are rewritten', 'Fields missing from older documents cannot be indexed'], a: 1, no: ['Joins are the cost of splitting data into tables. A document keeps its tree together, so one read returns it whole.', null, 'That is a risk of schema on write, where an ALTER TABLE can rewrite a big table. A document store just starts writing the new field.', 'Document stores can index a field that only some documents have; MongoDB, for one, indexes the rest as null or skips them with a sparse index.'], why: 'Schema on read moves the schema into the code. If some users have a name and others a first and last name, every reader must handle both, for good, unless you migrate the old documents after all. Schema on write has the database enforce one shape instead.' },
  // graphmodel: Graph Data Model
  { id: 'rv-graphmodel1', c: 'graphmodel', type: 'mc', q: 'A bank wants to flag accounts linked to a known fraudster through shared phones, cards or addresses, up to four links away. Which storage model fits that query best?', opts: ['A graph: accounts, phones and cards as vertices, sharing as edges', 'A search index over account fields, matching shared phone numbers', 'A document per account, embedding its phones, cards and addresses', 'A key-value store keyed by account ID, so each lookup is one fast read'], a: 0, no: [null, 'A search index finds accounts that share a value with the fraudster: one link. Chains four links deep need another round of searches per hop.', 'Documents keep one account\'s data together, but links between accounts are many-to-many. Each hop becomes another query, joined in application code.', 'Lookups by ID are fast, but this query is about the links between accounts. The app would fetch neighbours hop by hop, one round trip after another.'], why: 'Shared phones and cards connect accounts many-to-many, and the question follows those links a variable number of hops. A graph query does that in one pattern, like Cypher\'s [*1..4], without writing a join per hop.' },
  { id: 'rv-graphmodel2', c: 'graphmodel', type: 'mc', q: 'A "people you may know" feature walks friends of friends in a graph database. It answers in 20 ms for most users but times out for anyone connected to a celebrity with 40 million connections. Why?', opts: ['Graph databases cap edges per vertex, and this vertex is past it', 'Each hop is a join, and joins slow down as the edge table grows', 'The second hop walks through one vertex with 40 million edges', 'Friends of friends must scan every vertex in the graph to start'], a: 2, no: ['There is no such small cap; graph databases hold vertices with millions of edges. Storing them is fine; walking all of them in one query is not.', 'That would slow everyone, yet most users get 20 ms. A traversal\'s cost tracks how many edges it walks, not the size of the whole graph.', null, 'A traversal starts at the user\'s vertex, found through an index, and touches only what it reaches. That is why most users get 20 ms.'], why: 'A very high-degree vertex (a supernode) blows up any traversal that passes through it: one hop fans out to 40 million paths. Common fixes are to skip or cap such vertices in this query, sample their edges, or precompute suggestions for their neighbours offline.' },
  { id: 'rv-graphmodel3', c: 'graphmodel', type: 'mc', q: 'A team moves its order system to a graph database because orders link to customers and products. Its main queries are revenue per region per month and one customer\'s orders. Why is a graph a poor fit?', opts: ['New kinds of links between orders would each need a schema migration', 'Graph databases do not support transactions, so orders could be lost', 'Each hop costs a join, so even a one-customer lookup is slow', 'Its queries are aggregates and one-hop lookups, not deep traversals'], a: 3, no: ['Property graphs add new edge types without a schema change; that flexibility is one of their strengths, not the problem here.', 'Many graph databases are fully ACID; Neo4j is one. Transactions are not the issue: the shape of these queries is.', 'One customer\'s orders is a single hop, which a graph handles quickly. A graph does not lose on these lookups; it just gains nothing.', null], why: 'A graph earns its keep on many-hop traversals over many-to-many links. Revenue by region and month is a scan and aggregate over many rows, which SQL (or a column store, at scale) does better, and one customer\'s orders is an indexed lookup any database does well.' },
  // livecomments: Live Comments Fan-out
  { id: 'rv-livecomments1', c: 'livecomments', type: 'mc', q: 'Viewers of a live stream mostly read comments and post one now and then. How should each browser receive new comments and send its own?', opts: ['A WebSocket per viewer, since only WebSockets let the server push', 'A Server-Sent Events stream down, and a normal POST to comment', 'HTTP/2 server push, sending each new comment on the open connection', 'WebRTC data channels, so viewers relay comments to one another'], a: 1, no: ['SSE pushes too, over plain HTTP. A WebSocket works, but its two-way channel sits idle here; it earns its keep in chatty apps like games or shared editing.', null, 'HTTP/2 push sends files like CSS into the browser cache before they are asked for. Page code cannot listen to it, and Chrome has dropped it.', 'Peer-to-peer meshes do not scale to thousands of viewers, each relaying every comment. WebRTC suits live audio and video between a few peers.'], why: 'Traffic is almost all one way, so SSE fits: a long-lived HTTP response the server writes events into, with reconnects built into the browser. Posting stays an ordinary request, so it gets the usual auth, validation and rate limits.' },
  { id: 'rv-livecomments2', c: 'livecomments', type: 'mc', q: 'A realtime server holding 80,000 viewers\' comment streams crashes. Comments are saved before they are published. What should happen next?', opts: ['Pub/sub holds their comments until that server comes back up', 'A standby takes over the open connections where they left off', 'Viewers reconnect elsewhere and fetch what they missed by last ID', 'Each viewer reloads the video\'s whole comment history on reconnecting'], a: 2, no: ['Plain pub/sub, like Redis Pub/Sub, drops messages nobody is subscribed to, and the server may never return. Durable queues keep messages; here the database already does.', 'TCP connections die with the machine that held them, and a standby cannot simply resume them. Failover keeps the service up; clients still reconnect.', null, 'That reloads far more than was missed, and 80,000 viewers doing it at once hammers the database. Fetch only comments newer than the last one seen.'], why: 'The browser\'s EventSource reconnects on its own and sends the ID of the last event it got (Last-Event-ID). The new server subscribes to that video\'s channel and backfills newer comments from the store. Spread reconnects with random backoff so 80,000 clients do not arrive at once.' },
  { id: 'rv-livecomments3', c: 'livecomments', type: 'mc', q: '200 realtime servers, viewers assigned at random, 5,000 live videos with about 1,000 viewers each. Servers subscribe only to their viewers\' videos, yet every server gets every comment. What fixes it?', opts: ['Add more realtime servers, so each one holds fewer viewers', 'Switch viewers to WebSockets to cut the overhead per message', 'Route each video\'s viewers to a few servers by hashing its ID', 'Use an L4 load balancer so connections spread more evenly'], a: 2, no: ['Each server still has viewers of nearly every video, and each comment now reaches even more servers. The random spread is the problem, not the count.', 'The waste is between pub/sub and the servers, not on the viewer\'s connection. Per-message framing overhead is small either way.', null, 'An L4 balancer sees only IP addresses and ports, so it cannot route by video. Spreading evenly is what put every video on every server.'], why: 'A video with 1,000 random viewers lands on nearly all 200 servers, so per-video channels save nothing. An L7 load balancer (or a lookup) that hashes the video ID keeps each video\'s viewers on a few servers, which then subscribe to few channels. A hot video still gets spread wider.' },
  // eventsrc: Event Sourcing and CQRS
  { id: 'rv-eventsrc1', c: 'eventsrc', type: 'mc', q: 'An event-sourced shop appends OrderPlaced, then redirects the user to "My orders", which reads a projection. About one time in fifty the new order is missing. What is going on?', opts: ['The append failed silently, so the app should retry the command', 'The projection needs SERIALIZABLE isolation to see the new event', 'The projection only updates during its nightly rebuild from the log', 'The projection has not applied the event yet when the page reads'], a: 3, no: ['The append succeeded and the event is in the log. Retrying would place a second order, since placing one is not idempotent without an idempotency key.', 'Isolation levels order transactions inside one database. They cannot make a projection apply an event it has not received yet.', 'Then every order placed today would be missing, not one in fifty. Projections subscribe to the log and apply each event as it arrives.', null], why: 'In CQRS, read models lag the log a little, so a read straight after a write can miss it. Fixes: return the new event\'s position and have the read wait until the projection has reached it, or show the order from the command\'s own result.' },
  { id: 'rv-eventsrc2', c: 'eventsrc', type: 'mc', q: 'An event-sourced shop holds 40 million OrderPlaced events with an amount and no currency, from when it sold only in US dollars. It now sells in euros too. How should old and new events be handled?', opts: ['Rewrite the stored events in place, adding a currency to each', 'New events carry a currency; old ones are read as US dollars', 'Keep the event as is, and read every amount as euros from today', 'Keep the currency in the read models only, since they are rebuilt'], a: 1, no: ['Events record what happened; editing 40 million in place breaks the audit trail and disagrees with copies already read. A real migration copies and transforms into a new log.', null, 'Replays could no longer tell a dollar order from a euro one, so old totals would be misread. A change in meaning needs a new field or event version.', 'Read models are rebuilt from events, so a fact missing from the events is lost on the next rebuild. What happened belongs in the event.'], why: 'Events must keep their meaning for good, so a new field means a new version of the event. Old events stay untouched, and the code that reads them fills in what they lack (upcasting): no currency means US dollars.' },
  { id: 'rv-eventsrc3', c: 'eventsrc', type: 'mc', q: 'Your team has four new systems to build and time to event-source only one. Which gains the most from it?', opts: ['A payments ledger whose auditors trace how each balance came about', 'A session store for login tokens that expire after one hour', 'A product-page cache, rebuilt from the catalogue on every change', 'A user settings page, where only the latest choice ever matters'], a: 0, no: [null, 'Tokens live an hour and only the current one matters; nobody will replay them. A key-value store with a TTL, like Redis, fits.', 'A cache is already derived data, like a read model, so its history is worthless. Rebuild it from the catalogue, the real source of truth.', 'With no need for history, event sourcing only adds projections, replays and event versioning. Plain CRUD on a table does the job.'], why: 'Event sourcing pays off where the history itself is valuable: audits, disputes, "what was the balance on 3 March?". Where only the current state matters, its costs (projections, replays, versioning, careful deletes) buy nothing.' },
  // shred: Deleting from an Append-Only Log
  { id: 'rv-shred1', c: 'shred', type: 'mc', q: 'An event-sourced shop must keep every order\'s amount and date for seven years of accounting, but erase a customer\'s name and address on request. Which design fits?', opts: ['Encrypt the whole event log with one key held in a managed KMS', 'Give the event log a 30-day retention so personal data ages out', 'Encrypt personal fields with a per-customer key; delete it on request', 'Delete the customer\'s events, then rebuild each projection from the log'], a: 2, no: ['Encryption at rest protects stolen disks, but one shared key cannot forget one customer: deleting it erases every order for everyone.', 'Retention drops everyone\'s old events, including the amounts accounting must keep. It ages data out by time, not by person.', null, 'That rewrites history, breaks consumers and audits that trust the log, and loses the amounts accounting needs. Backups still hold the events too.'], why: 'Crypto-shredding can work per field: personal details are encrypted with the customer\'s own key, kept in a separate key store, while amounts and dates stay readable. Delete the key and the name and address are noise in every copy, backups included, and the books still add up. Keeping personal data out of the log, referenced by an ID, is the other common way.' },
  { id: 'rv-shred2', c: 'shred', type: 'mc', q: 'Each user\'s events are encrypted with their own key, and you delete the key when they ask to be forgotten. The key store is backed up nightly and backups are kept a year. What is the flaw?', opts: ['Restoring an old key-store backup would bring deleted keys back', 'The ciphertext is still in the log, so the data was never erased', 'Replicas of the log still hold plain-text copies of their events', 'Compaction must still remove their events before the delete counts'], a: 0, no: [null, 'Without the key, ciphertext is noise; that is the whole point of crypto-shredding. Old segments and backups can stay untouched.', 'Events are encrypted before they are appended, so replicas only ever copy ciphertext. Encrypting after replication would leave plain copies behind.', 'Deleting the key makes every copy unreadable at once. A tombstone and compaction just tidy the live log; they are not what erases the data.'], why: 'Crypto-shredding moves the problem into the key store: every copy of a key has to go, including the key store\'s own backups. It still needs backups, since losing it loses everyone\'s data, so keep them short-lived and a deleted key is gone everywhere within that window.' },
  { id: 'rv-shred3', c: 'shred', type: 'mc', q: 'Every user\'s events are encrypted with their own key, fetched from a key service. The key service is down for 20 minutes. What happens?', opts: ['Nothing: the events are already encrypted, so reads need no key', 'New events are written in plain text and encrypted once it is back', 'Every user\'s data becomes unreadable for good, as if shredded', 'Anything needing a key it has not cached fails until it is back'], a: 3, no: ['Encrypted data is useless until it is decrypted, and decrypting needs the key. That is exactly why deleting the key works.', 'Plain-text events would replicate and land in backups that no key deletion can reach. Fail closed instead: reject the write and let the client retry.', 'An outage is not a deletion: the keys still exist and work again once the service recovers. Only deleting a key shreds data.', null], why: 'Crypto-shredding puts the key service on the path of every read and write, so it needs the care of a database: replicas, and a short in-memory cache of keys. Keep that cache brief, because a deleted key stays usable until it expires from every cache.' },
  // failuredet: Failure Detection (Heartbeats and Indirect Probes)
  { id: 'rv-failuredet1', c: 'failuredet', type: 'mc', q: 'In a SWIM-style cluster, node A\'s direct ping to node C times out. A asks B and D to ping C for it, and both get replies and relay them to A. What should happen?', opts: ['C is marked suspect, since A\'s own direct ping got no reply', 'C stays alive; the fault is probably on the path from A to C', 'A is removed from the cluster, since it is the node dropping packets', 'The cluster stops taking writes until A and C can talk again'], a: 1, no: ['In SWIM, any reply relayed by another member counts as proof of life. Suspicion starts only when the direct ping and every indirect probe fail.', null, 'One bad path cannot say whether A, C or a switch between them is at fault, and A still reaches B and D. SWIM does not evict a member for that.', 'One broken link is not a partition of the cluster. Refusing writes is what the minority side of a real partition does in a quorum system.'], why: 'Indirect probes exist for exactly this: they separate "C is down" from "my route to C is down". Only when the direct ping and every indirect probe fail does C become suspect, then dead after a suspicion timeout with no news, and gossip spreads the verdict.' },
  { id: 'rv-failuredet2', c: 'failuredet', type: 'mc', q: 'A cluster declares a node dead after 500 ms without a heartbeat reply. A traffic spike slows every node\'s replies to about 800 ms, though none has crashed. What happens?', opts: ['Busy but healthy nodes are declared dead, and their load piles onto the rest', 'No node is removed, since slow replies still arrive and count as heartbeats', 'The slowest nodes are dropped, which sheds load so that the rest recover', 'Real crashes go unnoticed, since every node\'s replies now look the same'], a: 0, no: [null, 'A reply after the deadline is too late: the node was already counted as missed. A timeout below normal worst-case latency turns slowness into death.', 'Dropping servers removes capacity, so the survivors get busier and slower. Load shedding drops low-priority requests, not the servers that serve them.', 'A crashed node would be declared dead too, along with everyone else. A short timeout misses nothing; it adds false alarms.'], why: 'A failure detector only sees silence, and under load healthy nodes go quiet too. Each false failover moves load to the survivors, which slow down and get declared dead in turn: a cascade. Set timeouts above worst-case latency under load, add a suspicion period, or use an adaptive detector (phi accrual) that learns how late heartbeats normally are.' },
  { id: 'rv-failuredet3', c: 'failuredet', type: 'mc', q: 'To stop false failovers, a team raises its heartbeat timeout from 5 to 60 seconds. Clients use whichever nodes the detector lists as alive. Then a node loses power. What happens?', opts: ['Nothing visible: its replicas take over the moment it goes silent', 'A short blip: its TCP connections reset, so clients retry elsewhere', 'Requests sent to it hang or fail for up to a minute', 'Split brain: it may keep acting as leader for that whole minute'], a: 2, no: ['Replicas take over only once the detector declares it dead, so for up to 60 seconds traffic still goes to the dead node.', 'A machine without power sends nothing, not even a reset. Its connections just go quiet, and clients wait for their own timeouts.', null, 'A powered-off node cannot act at all. Split brain comes from a node wrongly declared dead while still running, which long timeouts make rarer.'], why: 'Every timeout trades false alarms against slow detection. At 60 seconds, a real crash leaves a dead node listed as alive for up to a minute. Clients can soften it with short request timeouts and a retry on another node, but detection itself is now slow.' },
  // postmortem: Blameless Postmortems
  { id: 'rv-postmortem1', c: 'postmortem', type: 'mc', q: 'A postmortem\'s five whys stops at "the on-call engineer missed the alert". That alert fires about 40 times a day, nearly always for nothing. What should it conclude?', opts: ['Human error: retrain the engineer to look at every alert that fires', 'Lower the alert\'s threshold so it fires earlier and is harder to miss', 'Page two engineers for it, so that one of them is sure to respond', 'The noisy alert is the cause: make it page only on real user impact'], a: 3, no: ['Human error is where the five whys starts. Forty false alarms a day would train anyone to tune it out, so retraining one person leaves the trap for the next.', 'A lower threshold makes it fire even more often, so it is ignored even more. Good alerts page on symptoms users feel.', 'Two people tune out the same noise, and now both are paged 40 times a day. A secondary is for escalating real pages that go unanswered.', null], why: 'Keep asking why until you reach something the system can change. An alert that is nearly always wrong trains people to ignore it (alert fatigue). The action item is to fix the alert, with an owner and a date, so it pages only when users are affected.' },
  { id: 'rv-postmortem2', c: 'postmortem', type: 'mc', q: 'After an outage, a company disciplines the engineer whose config change triggered it. What is the most likely effect on future incidents?', opts: ['Fewer incidents, since engineers take more care with every change', 'Mistakes get reported late or hidden, so incidents run longer', 'The same failure can\'t recur, since that engineer now knows the risk', 'Postmortems stay accurate, since the logs record who did what anyway'], a: 1, no: ['Care does not close the gap that let one config change take production down. Without validation, review or a canary, the next person makes the same mistake.', null, 'One person learned; the system did not. Anyone else can push the same change until a check, a canary or a safer default stops it.', 'Logs show what happened, not why it made sense at the time. That context comes from people, who stop sharing it once honesty gets punished.'], why: 'People who fear blame hide what happened, or wait before raising the alarm, and then nobody learns. The trigger was one person\'s change; the root cause is a system that let it reach production unchecked. A blameless postmortem fixes that system.' },
  { id: 'rv-postmortem3', c: 'postmortem', type: 'mc', q: 'A team writes a full postmortem for every page. Most pages are alerts that cleared on their own with no user impact, and nobody reads the write-ups. What is a better rule?', opts: ['Write one when users were hurt, data was lost or a human stepped in', 'Write one only when an outage breaches the monthly SLO', 'Write one only when the root cause turns out to be a code change', 'Write one only when someone outside the team asks for it'], a: 0, no: [null, 'That misses data loss, slow detection and near misses saved by a rollback, which can all happen inside the SLO. Those are often the cheapest lessons you get.', 'Config pushes, expired certificates, full disks and capacity limits cause many outages. Narrowing to code changes misses them.', 'Then learning depends on who happened to notice. Incidents nobody outside the team saw, like a rollback that saved the day, still teach a lot.'], why: 'Postmortems take real time, so set clear triggers. The Google SRE book lists user-visible impact, any data loss, an on-call intervention such as a rollback, a long time to resolve and a monitoring failure. A page that clears itself with no impact is a bad alert to fix, not an incident.' },
  // colstore: Column Storage & Analytics (OLTP vs OLAP)
  { id: 'rv-colstore1', c: 'colstore', type: 'mc', q: 'A warehouse holds 2 billion sale events. Analysts want revenue by product category, store region and month, in any combination. How should the tables be laid out?', opts: ['Copy the app\'s 30 normalised tables as they are and join them per query', 'A document database, with each sale\'s product and store nested inside', 'One fact table of sales, with small product, store and date tables', 'A key-value store keyed by sale ID, holding each sale as one value'], a: 2, no: ['That schema avoids duplicate data so the app\'s small writes stay simple. Analytic queries over it chain many joins across billions of rows.', 'Document stores read whole documents. Grouping 2 billion of them means reading every field of every sale: the row-store problem again.', null, 'A key-value store answers "give me sale 123", which nobody here asks. It cannot group or filter by category without reading every value.'], why: 'This is a star schema: the fact table holds one row per sale with the numbers (amount, quantity) and IDs pointing to dimension tables (product, store, date) that hold the descriptions. Queries filter on the dimensions and add up the facts, each dimension one join away, and a column store reads only the few columns involved.' },
  { id: 'rv-colstore2', c: 'colstore', type: 'mc', q: 'A nightly job copies the full 2 TB orders table from a read replica into the warehouse. It now takes 9 hours and grows every month. What fixes it for good?', opts: ['Index every column on the replica so the full read runs faster', 'Run the job weekly instead, so it has a whole weekend to finish', 'Move the job to a bigger server with more CPU and more memory', 'Copy only rows changed since the last run, by timestamp or change log'], a: 3, no: ['Indexes find a few rows fast; reading all 2 TB still reads all 2 TB. Each index also adds work to every write the database takes.', 'Reports go a week stale, and a job that still copies everything keeps growing until a weekend is not enough either.', 'Scaling up buys a few months, but a full copy still grows with the table. The fix is to stop re-reading rows that never changed.', null], why: 'Incremental loads copy only what changed: rows with an updated_at after the last run, or better, the database\'s change log (change data capture). CDC also catches deletes, which a timestamp misses, and can keep the warehouse minutes behind instead of a day.' },
  { id: 'rv-colstore3', c: 'colstore', type: 'mc', q: 'A warehouse table of events is sorted by event time. Queries over the last 7 days are fast, but one filtering on a single customer_id across all time is slow. Why?', opts: ['A column store reads every column of each row to test one filter', 'Blocks can be skipped by their time range, but not by customer ID', 'It looks up the rows through a B-tree index, one disk seek per row', 'It must join every event to the customers table before filtering'], a: 1, no: ['Column stores read only the columns a query touches: here customer_id first, then the other columns for the few rows that match.', null, 'Most columnar warehouses keep no B-tree on a column like this; they scan it. A real index lookup for a few rows would be fast, not slow.', 'The filter needs no join: customer_id sits right in the fact table. Joins to small dimension tables are cheap in a star schema anyway.'], why: 'Each block of rows records the min and max of its columns. Sorted by time, a 7-day filter skips nearly every block; customer IDs are mixed through all of them, so that filter scans the whole customer_id column. A sort key is a trade-off: some warehouses let you cluster on a second key or keep a second copy sorted another way.' },
  // oncall: On-Call, SLOs and Error Budgets
  { id: 'rv-oncall1', c: 'oncall', type: 'mc', q: 'One service runs interactive search, where users wait on each request, and a nightly bulk export that customers pick up at 8 a.m. How should you set its SLOs?', opts: ['One availability SLO: the share of all requests that succeed', 'One p99 latency SLO across both kinds of request, for one number', 'Separate SLOs: latency for search, finish time for the exports', 'Only a search SLO, as the exports run while customers are asleep'], a: 2, no: ['A search that answers in 8 seconds still counts as a success, and so does an export that finishes at noon. Availability alone misses what these users feel.', 'Exports take minutes by design and searches take milliseconds. One latency target is wrong for one of them, and its problems hide in the mix.', null, 'Customers rely on the export at 8 a.m. A late or missing export is a failure they notice, just later than a slow search.'], why: 'An SLI should match what each kind of user feels. Request-driven work like search is judged on latency and errors; batch work like an export is judged on freshness or finishing on time, such as 99% of exports ready by 8 a.m. One blended number hides problems in both.' },
  { id: 'rv-oncall2', c: 'oncall', type: 'mc', q: 'A checkout outage pages the primary on-call at 3 a.m., but their phone is on silent. Twenty minutes pass with no response. What should the paging setup have done?', opts: ['Paged the primary again every minute until they acknowledged it', 'Escalated to the secondary when the page went unacknowledged', 'Posted the alert in the team chat channel for whoever was still awake', 'Rolled back the latest deploy on its own, so no human was needed'], a: 1, no: ['A phone on silent stays silent, and the outage runs on. Repeating the page to one person adds no second responder; escalation does.', null, 'At 3 a.m. nobody may be awake, and a chat message wakes nobody. Chat is for coordinating an incident, not for making sure someone answers.', 'That only helps if a deploy caused it. Checkout can fail from a dependency, a full disk or a traffic spike, and a human still has to look.'], why: 'The secondary exists to catch missed pages. An escalation policy pages the next person when a page goes unacknowledged for a few minutes, so a silent phone, a flat battery or a dead zone never leaves an outage with nobody on it.' },
  { id: 'rv-oncall3', c: 'oncall', type: 'mc', q: 'Product asks for a 99.99% monthly availability SLO on an internal reporting dashboard that analysts open a few times a day. What does agreeing cost the team?', opts: ['A budget of about 4 minutes a month, so one bad deploy freezes releases', 'Little: an SLO only changes the target line drawn on the team\'s graphs', 'A matching 99.99% SLA, which must be signed off before the SLO counts', 'Riskier launches, since a higher SLO gives the team a bigger budget'], a: 0, no: [null, 'The SLO drives the alerts and the release policy. A tighter one means more pages and more freezes, not just a new line on a graph.', 'An SLO needs no SLA, and most internal services have none. Where an SLA exists, it is set looser than the SLO, so you see trouble before you owe anyone.', 'Backwards: the budget is 1 minus the SLO, so each extra nine divides it by ten. 99.99% leaves a tenth of what 99.9% does.'], why: '99.99% of a 30-day month is about 4.3 minutes of allowed failure. One bad deploy or a slow failover spends it, and the budget policy then freezes releases, all for a dashboard whose users would not notice a few minutes a month. Set the SLO where users stay happy, not higher.' },
  // srealert: Monitoring and Alerting
  { id: 'rv-srealert1', c: 'srealert', type: 'mc', q: 'Your service pages only when p99 latency goes above 500 ms. A bad deploy makes 30% of requests fail with HTTP 500 in about 5 ms each. What happens?', opts: ['A page within minutes, since failed requests are counted as timeouts', 'A page, as the load balancer marks the failing servers as down', 'No page: the failures are fast, so latency stays normal or drops', 'A page, as the extra error handling pushes CPU and latency up'], a: 2, no: ['A 500 returned in 5 ms is not a timeout. It lands in the fastest part of the latency histogram, far from the p99.', 'A load balancer pulling servers pages nobody, and its health check may still pass while real requests fail. Errors need their own alert.', null, 'Failing in 5 ms is cheaper than doing the real work, so CPU and latency are more likely to fall. Fast failures look like a faster service.'], why: 'Latency is only one of the four golden signals. Fast failures pull the average down and leave p99 about where it was, so a latency-only alert sleeps through a big outage. Watch errors too, track the latency of failed requests separately, and page on the error-budget burn.' },
  { id: 'rv-srealert2', c: 'srealert', type: 'mc', q: 'Users report checkout won\'t load. Your server metrics show 0.01% errors and normal latency, but traffic is down 40%. What monitoring would have paged on this?', opts: ['A lower error threshold, so 0.01% on the servers is enough to page', 'Per-host CPU and memory alerts, to catch a struggling server early', 'Logging every request in full on the servers, instead of sampling', 'Black-box probes from outside that load checkout the way users do'], a: 3, no: ['0.01% is normal noise, and the failing requests never reach the servers at all. No threshold on server errors can count them.', 'The servers are healthy; they just are not getting the requests. CPU and memory are causes, and here they look fine.', 'Servers can only log requests that arrive. Whatever fails before them, in DNS, TLS or the load balancer, leaves no line.', null], why: 'Server-side (white-box) metrics only see requests that reach the servers. If DNS, an expired TLS certificate or the load balancer breaks, users fail while your servers see a quiet day. Black-box probes test the service as users do, and a sudden drop in traffic is itself a symptom worth alerting on.' },
  { id: 'rv-srealert3', c: 'srealert', type: 'mc', q: 'Your team pages only on symptoms users feel. Which of these causes, with users still fine for now, deserves a page at 3 a.m. anyway?', opts: ['At the current rate, the primary database disk fills in two hours', 'One of twelve web servers failed its health checks and left the pool', 'A TLS certificate expires in ten days, and its auto-renewal failed', 'Replication lag on the analytics replica has reached twenty minutes'], a: 0, no: [null, 'The load balancer already routed around it, and eleven servers carry the load. Replace it in the morning, or let automation do it.', 'Real, but ten days away: a ticket for working hours. Fix the renewal job (for example ACME) well before the certificate lapses.', 'Analytics can run twenty minutes behind without hurting users. Watch it on a dashboard, and ticket it if it keeps growing.'], why: 'Paging on symptoms is the rule, but a cause that will become a user-facing outage before anyone could act in the morning is the exception. A full primary disk stops writes, so wake someone now. The SRE Book counts predictions like "the disk fills in 4 hours" as part of saturation.' },
  // sretoil: Eliminating Toil
  { id: 'rv-sretoil1', c: 'sretoil', type: 'mc', q: 'Database failover runs from a script on one engineer\'s laptop, and only she knows how to use it. She starts a month of leave next week. What is the best next step?', opts: ['Write a runbook of the script\'s steps, so others can do them by hand', 'Turn the script into a shared, tested tool anyone on call can run', 'Copy the script onto every on-call engineer\'s laptop before she goes', 'Plan any failovers for the weeks when she is back at work and available'], a: 1, no: ['That steps down the ladder: slower, and easier to get wrong at 3 a.m. A runbook is where automation starts, not where it should end up.', null, 'Copies drift apart, nobody tests or maintains them, and nobody knows which is current. It is still a personal script, just several of them.', 'Failovers happen when a database breaks, not on a schedule. The month she is away is exactly when one might be needed.'], why: 'This is the SRE Book\'s own example of the automation ladder: failover by hand, a script in one person\'s home directory, a shared tool everyone uses, then a database that fails over by itself. Moving up one rung removes the single person; the top rung removes the task.' },
  { id: 'rv-sretoil2', c: 'sretoil', type: 'mc', q: 'Each new customer needs 30 minutes of the same manual setup from your ops team. You sign up 40 a week, and sign-ups double every year. What happens if it stays manual?', opts: ['Little: half an hour per customer stays a small part of the week', 'It is safe as long as every setup step is written down in a runbook', 'The team gets quicker with practice, so the total stays about flat', 'It grows from 20 hours a week to 80 in two years, crowding out engineering'], a: 3, no: ['40 setups at 30 minutes is already 20 hours a week, half an engineer. Small tasks add up when they scale with customers.', 'A runbook makes the setup consistent, but it is still done by hand and still doubles with sign-ups. It is the first rung, not the fix.', 'Practice trims a few minutes once, while the number of setups keeps doubling. Work that scales with customers outgrows any speed-up.', null], why: 'That is toil: manual, repetitive, automatable, and growing with the service. 20 hours a week becomes 40 next year and 80 the year after, until the team does nothing else. Automate it, with self-service sign-up or a provisioning pipeline, so the cost stays flat as customers grow.' },
  { id: 'rv-sretoil3', c: 'sretoil', type: 'mc', q: 'A script drains and reimages any server that fails its health check. A bug in the check flags 90% of the fleet at once. What safeguard should the automation have had?', opts: ['A human approval click before each server is drained and reimaged', 'Faster reimaging, so that flagged servers rejoin the pool sooner', 'A cap on how much of the fleet it can touch before a human checks', 'A full audit log of every action, so the team can review it later'], a: 2, no: ['That brings the toil back as hundreds of clicks a week, and people soon approve on reflex. Guard the rare dangerous case, not every routine one.', 'Even a fast reimage takes 90% of capacity away at once, and the rest overloads. Speed does not limit how much breaks.', null, 'A log tells you what happened after the outage. It is good practice, but it stops nothing while the script runs.'], why: 'Automation works fast and at scale, so a bug hits everything at once. Give it limits: act on a small share of the fleet at a time, stop and page a human when far more than usual looks broken, and sanity-check its inputs. Then it removes toil without becoming the outage.' },
  // srecascade: Overload and Cascading Failures
  { id: 'rv-srecascade1', c: 'srecascade', type: 'mc', q: 'Product pages call a required inventory service and an optional recommendations service. Recommendations is overloaded and slow, and now product pages time out too. Best fix?', opts: ['Retry each slow recommendations call up to three times so more succeed', 'Time out recommendations quickly and render the page without them', 'Put a large queue in front of recommendations, so no call is ever lost', 'Add more product page servers, since they are the ones timing out now'], a: 1, no: ['Retries add load to a service that is already overloaded, and each one makes the page wait longer. Retries suit brief blips, not overload.', null, 'Calls wait in the queue instead, and pages still hang on them. A queue smooths short bursts; it does not add capacity.', 'Their threads are stuck waiting on recommendations. More servers just send more calls to the overloaded service.'], why: 'An optional dependency must not take down the page. A short timeout, or a circuit breaker that stops calling it while it struggles, lets the page degrade gracefully without recommendations, and takes load off the service so it can recover.' },
  { id: 'rv-srecascade2', c: 'srecascade', type: 'mc', q: 'Four servers behind a load balancer each run at 80% of capacity. One crashes, and the load balancer spreads its traffic over the other three. What happens next?', opts: ['Each survivor now needs about 107% of its capacity, so they may fail too', 'Each survivor rises to about 85%, which is busy but still within limits', 'Each survivor hits exactly 100%, which is fully used but still stable', 'Traffic drops by a quarter, as the crashed server\'s users get errors'], a: 0, no: [null, 'The arithmetic is off: four servers at 80% is 320% of one server, and split over three that is about 107% each, not 85%.', '320% over three servers is about 107% each, not 100%. And even near 100%, queues build and latency climbs steeply.', 'Only until the load balancer drops it from the pool, within seconds. Then all of its traffic lands on the three that are left.'], why: '4 x 80% = 320% of one server\'s capacity, now on three servers: about 107% each. Overloaded servers slow down, fail health checks or run out of memory, and their load moves on again: a cascade. To survive losing one of four, run each below 75%, and lower in practice, since latency climbs well before 100%.' },
  { id: 'rv-srecascade3', c: 'srecascade', type: 'mc', q: 'Your client retries failed calls with exponential backoff and jitter. For which of these failures is retrying the wrong choice?', opts: ['503 Service Unavailable, sent with a Retry-After header of 30 seconds', 'A connection reset before any response came back, on a GET request', '429 Too Many Requests, after a burst of traffic from this one client', '400 Bad Request, saying a required field in the body is missing'], a: 3, no: ['A 503 says the server is briefly unable to cope. Waiting the 30 seconds it asks for, then retrying, is exactly right.', 'The call never got an answer, and GET is idempotent, so trying again is safe. A POST would need an idempotency key first.', 'A 429 means slow down, not stop. Back off, honour any Retry-After, and the retry can then succeed.', null], why: 'A 400 is a permanent error: the same request fails the same way every time, so each retry is pure extra load on a service that may already be struggling. Retry only transient failures, such as timeouts, resets, 503s and 429s after waiting.' }
);
